nced distribution of colors among vertices, they should be grouped by packets\nof n −1. Hence the separation number must be (the ceiling of) a certain function of a, b, p divided\nby n −1. By Property 1, this function must also be close to an affine function. Second, supported\nby Proposition 3.1, we conjecture that when a = pb, then sep(Kn, a, b) = a(p −1)/(n −1). All\nthis together lead us to propose the above conjecture, with ϵ being the correcting term depending\non a, b, n.\nIn particular, for n = 4, the following refinement is conjectured, where only the case 2b ≤a <\n3b remains to be verified:\nConjecture 3.\n⌊2(a−b)\n3\n⌋,\nb ≤a < 2b,\n⌈4a−6b−1\n\n\n\n\nsep(K4, a, b) =\n3\n⌉,\n2b ≤a < 3b,\n2a −4b,\n3b ≤a < 4b,\na,\na ≥4b.\n\n\n\n69\nwww.ejgta.org--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColorSym optimality is false | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/godin-grisot-togni-2022-colorsym-optimality/)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Jean-Christophe Godin, Rémi Grisot and Olivier Togni, On List Coloring with Separation of the Complete Graph and Set System Intersections, arXiv:2209.03436v1, Section 3.1; Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications 13(1), 57–74, DOI 10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5, Section 3.1, p. ... There is no separation bound in the conjecture. ... Godin–Togni, JGAA 29(1), DOI 10.7155/jgaa.v29i1.3096, concerns multicoloring handles rather than ColorSym.\n\n# ColorSym optimality is false\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/ConceptDynamics/GraphColoring/ColorSymOptimalityRefutation.result10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nJean-Christophe Godin, Rémi Grisot and Olivier Togni, On List Coloring with Separation of the Complete Graph and Set System Intersections, arXiv:2209.03436v1, Section 3.1; Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications 13(1), 57–74, DOI 10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5, Section 3.1, p. 63:\n\n> We conjecture that this algorithm is optimal (i.e., produces an (L, b)-coloring if K_n is (L, b)-colorable).\n\nThe algorithm is ColorSym. An $a$-list assignment associates to every vertex a set of $a$ integers. An $(L,b)$-coloring assigns a $b$-element subset of each vertex's list, with disjoint subsets at adjacent vertices. For $K_n$ every pair of distinct vertices is adjacent.\n\n| `AdmissibleOrder` and `runSupports`. | Every support of size at least two occurs once; sizes are nondecreasing; equal-size order is arbitrary. | | “Remove a color from Ip(S) and assign it to vj.” | `AdmissiblePick` and `assign`. | Every choice from a nonempty integer pool is allowed; insertion and load increment are explicit. | | “let j ∈ S be the index for which w(j) is minimum (if more than one j with minimum w, then take the smallest index)” | `best`. | The minimum-load vertices are sorted by index and the first is selected. | | “If wi ≥ b for each i or i = n + 1, then Stop.” | `stopped`, `runPool` and the terminal case of `runSupports`. | Global stopping is rechecked at each color assignment; exhausting the finite support order implements the terminal step. | | “Goto Step i until Ip(S) is empty.” | `runPool`. | The pool loses one chosen color per assignment; initial fuel is its cardinality. | | “Color every proper intersections of sets of size 1: vi gets |Ip({i})| colors. Thus wi = |Ip({i})|.” | `initial`. | Singleton atoms determine both the initial outputs and loads. | | “produces an (L, b)-coloring if K_n is (L, b)-colorable” | `colorSym`, `claim` and `result`. | The claim asks for some admissible order and pick whose output contains a valid $b$-coloring; `result` negates it. |\n\nThe existential choice of order and pick and the allowance to trim an output to $b$ colors give the algorithm the weakest favorable reading. Every admissible execution fails on the witness, so this allowance does not rescue the conjecture. The fuel parameter, option for an empty support, product state and shifted indices implement the algorithm; they impose no additional hypothesis on the public claim. There is no separation bound in the conjecture.\n\nMotivation\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOlivier Togni Research/Publications (https://o.togni.u-bourgogne.fr/index.php?n=Research.Publications)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Togni, Completely Independent Spanning Trees in Split Graphs: Structural Properties and Complexity, Discrete Applied Mathematics 391, 511-522 (2026). arXiv:2512.15486 ... Godin, O. Togni, New reducible configurations for graph multicoloring with application to the experimental resolution of McDiarmid-Reed's Conjecture, Journal of Graph Algorithms and Applications 29(1) 267–288 (2025) . arXiv:1812.01911 ... Grisot, O. Togni, On List Coloring with Separation of the Complete Graph and Set System Intersections, Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications 13(1), 2025, 57-74. arXiv:2209.03436\n\nIf you have no access to one of the references below, feel free to contact me (otogni@u-bourgogne.fr)\n\nSubmitted papers\n\n  1. M. Lalou, N. Mbarek, A. Skender, O. Togni, Constructing two completely independent spanning trees in dual-cubes, Submitted .\n\n  2. H. AlBazzal and O. Togni, t-tone colorings of outerplanar and Halin graphs, Submitted .\n\nInternational journal papers\n\n  1. M. Mortada and O. Togni, On S-packing Coloring of Bounded Degree Graphs, Ars Mathematica Contemporanea, accepted, 2026 .\n\n  2. M. Lalou, N. Mbarek, A. Skender, O. Togni, Completely Independent Spanning Trees in Split Graphs: Structural Properties and Complexity, Discrete Applied Mathematics 391, 511-522 (2026). arXiv:2512.15486\n\n  3. J.C. Godin, O. Togni, New reducible configurations for graph multicoloring with application to the experimental resolution of McDiarmid-Reed's Conjecture, Journal of Graph Algorithms and Appli