G$ and integers $a,b$, find the largest integer $c$ such that for any list assignment $L$ of $G$ with $|L(v)|= a$ for any vertex $v$ and $|L(u)\\cap L(v)|\\le c$ for any edge $uv$ of $G$, there exists an assignment $\\varphi$ of sets of integers to the vertices of $G$ such that $\\varphi(u)\\subset L(u)$ and $|\\varphi(v)|=b$ for any vertex $u$ and $\\varphi(u)\\cap \\varphi(v)=\\emptyset$ for any edge $uv$. Such a value of $c$ is called the separation number of $(G,a,b)$. Using a special partition of a set of lists for which we obtain an improved version of Poincaré's crible, we determine the separation number of the complete graph $K_n$ for some values of $a,b$ and $n$, and prove bounds for the remaining values.\n\n  * Analytic Classification of Generic Unfoldings of Antiholomorphic Parabolic Fixed Points of Codimension 1\n\nJonathan Godin, Christiane Rousseau\n\nMay 24 2021 math.DS math.CV arXiv:2105.10348v1\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn List Coloring with Separation of the Complete Graph and Set System Intersections (https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.03436)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Authors: Jean-Christophe Godin, Rémi Grisot, Olivier Togni\nDate: Wed Sep  7 19:42:50 2022 ... Using a special partition of a set of lists for which we obtain an improved version of Poincar\\'e's crible, we determine the separation number of the complete graph $K_n$ for some values of $a,b$ and $n$, and prove bounds for the remaining values.\nTitle: On List Coloring with Separation of the Complete Graph and Set System Intersections\nAuthors: Jean-Christophe Godin, Rémi Grisot, Olivier Togni\nDate: Wed Sep  7 19:42:50 2022\n\nWe consider the following list coloring with separation problem: Given a graph $G$ and integers $a,b$, find the largest integer $c$ such that for any list assignment $L$ of $G$ with $|L(v)|= a$ for any vertex $v$ and $|L(u)\\cap L(v)|\\le c$ for any edge $uv$ of $G$, there exists an assignment $\\varphi$ of sets of integers to the vertices of $G$ such that $\\varphi(u)\\subset L(u)$ and $|\\varphi(v)|=b$ for any vertex $u$ and $\\varphi(u)\\cap \\varphi(v)=\\emptyset$ for any edge $uv$. Such a value of $c$ is called the separation number of $(G,a,b)$. Using a special partition of a set of lists for which we obtain an improved version of Poincar\\'e's crible, we determine the separation number of the complete graph $K_n$ for some values of $a,b$ and $n$, and prove bounds for the remaining values.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Math and Combinatorics Seminar - Department of Mathematics | University of South Carolina (https://travelandtourism.sc.edu/study/colleges_schools/artsandsciences/mathematics/beyond_classroom/colloquia_and_seminars/discrete_math_seminar/index.php)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 3/6 - Graphs from Permutations and the Higher Bruhat Orders - Kevin Lui ... An extension of it to list coloring requires only that the size of every color class is at most \\( \\lceil n/k \\rceil \\). ... This continues the line of research initiated by Faudree, Gy\\'arf\\'as, Lesniak, and Schelp, and Mubayi and Stading, who studied the case \\( k = q \\), and by Conder, who considered the case \\( k = 6 \\) and \\( q = 2 \\). ... In this paper, we prove that the threshold for the existence of a perfect \\(K_r\\)-tiling of a randomly perturbed balanced \\(r\\)-partite graph on \\(rn\\) vertices is \\(n^{-2/r}\\). This result is a multipartite analog of a theorem of Balogh, Treglown, and Wagner and is joint work with Enrique Gomez-Leos.\n\n3/6 - Graphs from Permutations and the Higher Bruhat Orders - Kevin Lui\n\nWhen: March 6, 2026 at 2:30 p.m.\nSpeaker: Kevin Lui\nLocation: LeConte 346\n\nAbstract: Several graphs arise in the study of the symmetric group \\( S_n \\), with two prominent ones including the graph on \\( S_n \\) generated by adjacent transpositions and the reduced word graph for each element of \\( S_n \\). In this talk, we will present a generalization of the Higher Bruhat Orders originally due to Manin and Schechtman, and this generalization contains the aforementioned graphs as special cases. Our main results directly extend those in the classical Higher Bruhat Orders. As a consequence, we obtain several structural properties on the quotient of a reduced word graph by commutations. These apply to arbitrary elements of \\( S_n \\), as well as a more general setting called the affine symmetric group. This talk is based on joint work with Sara Billey and Herman Chau.\n\n3/20 - An introduction to equitable DP coloring of graphs - Zimu Xiang\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGaruda - Garba Rujukan Digital (https://garuda.kemdiktisaintek.go.id/journal/view/5456?page=36)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; In this paper, we explore the Hamiltonicity of directed Toeplitz graphs of the form Tn⟨1, 3, 6; t⟩. ... On list coloring with separation of the complete graph and set system intersections Godin, Jean-Christophe; Grisot, Rémi; Togni, Olivier\nPublisher : GTA Research Group, Univ. Newcastle, Indonesian Combinatorics Society and ITB\n\nShow Abstract | Download Original | Original Source | Check in Google Scholar | DOI: 10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.10\n\n#### Abstract\n\nA directed Toeplitz graph, denoted as Tn⟨s1, …, sk; t1, …, tl⟩ of order n, is a digraph in which an edge (i,  j) exists if and only if j − i = sp or i − j = tq for some 1 ≤ p ≤ k and 1 ≤ q ≤ l. The adjacency matrix of such a graph forms a Toeplitz matrix, characterized by constant values along all diagonals parallel to the main diagonal. In this paper, we explore the Hamiltonicity of directed Toeplitz graphs of the form Tn⟨1, 3, 6; t⟩. We establish that Tn⟨1, 3, 6; t⟩ is Hamiltonian for t = 5, 10 and for all t ≥ 12, for every n. Additionally, we show that the graph remains Hamiltonian for all n, with only a finite number of exceptions when t = 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 11. Specifically, for t = 1, the graph is Hamiltonian only when n = 7, while for t = 2, it is Hamiltonian under certain conditions on n, namely when n ≡ 0, 1, 3 (mod 4). \n\nOn list coloring with separation of the complete graph and set system intersections Godin, Jean-Christophe; Grisot, Rémi; Togni, Olivier\nElectronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications (EJGTA) Vol 13, No 1 (2025): Electronic Journal of Graph Theory and Applications\nPublisher : GTA Research Group, Univ. Newcastle, Indonesian Combinatorics Society and ITB\n\nShow Abstract | Download Original | Original Source | Check in Google Scholar | DOI: 10.5614/ejgta.2025.13.1.5\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Jean-Christophe Godin (https://dblp.org/pid/72/9703.html)\nciteturn7search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Jean-Christophe Godin, Rémi Grisot Image: 0000-0001-9652-5618, Olivier Togni Image: 0000-0001-9510-3595:\nOn list coloring with separation of the complete graph and set system intersections. ... 13(1): 57-74 (2025) ... New Reducible Configurations for Graph Multicoloring with Application to the Experimental Resolution of McDiarmid-Reed's Conjecture.\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/ejgta/GodinGT25\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/ejgta/GodinGT25\n\nJean-Christophe Godin, Rémi GrisotImage: 0000-0001-9652-5618, Olivier TogniImage: 0000-0001-9510-3595:\nOn list coloring with separation of the complete grap